Two ideas, actually, but both pretty minor.

I think you (your character) should have at least some rough awareness
of how your memory for spells is doing.  The spell list you get when
you hit Z should include some indication of whether the spell was
learned recently, long ago, very long ago, or is near being forgotten.
Or, it could just give you a counter and you'd know exactly.  I think
this falls under the category of "anything the player can do with
tedious use of pen and paper, is just a convenience."

It wouldn't affect the game much but make it a little more smoothly
playable, since you wouldn't have to try to keep track of when you
read your spellbooks out of band.  Potential drawback: It might give
away a bit of a spoiler to new players who have never forgotten a
spell and don't realize there's a memory duration limit on spells.

Second, and this also wouldn't affect gameplay much, but I've long
thought that a helm of brilliance should affect how well you can
remember spells.  Perhaps each point of enchantment would be worth an
extra 2000 turns of memory, so with a +5 helm you'd remember spells
for 30000 turns.

This would be trivial to implement if the game kept track of spell
memory by noting which turn you last read each spell, and comparing
current time to spell read time when you try to cast it.  Instead, the
game seems to give you a memory counter for each spell, which gets
decremented each turn.  And makes a feature like this mildly more
annoying to code, since it means each time you put on, take off,
enchant, disenchant, or damage the helmet, it'd have to go through all
your spells adjusting the counter.  Why is it coded that way, anyway?

(I realize it would have to use signed counters if it worked the other
way, since reading a spellbook when you already know the spell would
mean setting the "when was it read" timer to a point in the near
future, but overall it seems like a much simpler way of doing it...)

